  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.6 z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times the loss. It is steeper in x (left to right). Plain steps overshoot in x when the rate is above 1/2.4 ≈ 0.42 and swings grow above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83.",
    "Each step is -rate × (true slope + noise). The noise has spread 1.5 × sqrt((64 − b)/(63 b)) on each axis, which is 1.5 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 5 times their real length, using the same scale. Purple is the mini-batch step the ball takes next. Dark is the step the full data would take, against the true slope.",
    "A run finishes after 40 steps. It is marked diverged when the loss goes past 6, about the bowl's rim. Points off the bowl are drawn at the rim, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus up to 40 step points, and the ghost holds the same. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence. A new run uses the next noise values."
  ]
}